                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7b9df209-f199-4ae8-b0a4-4f4e879fdf26",
                        "source_entry_id": "9917ab7e-0b7a-3734-a837-e6f99b3d3b88",
                        "target_lang_word": "preimage",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "preimage",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "57f30964-aa7b-34fb-816b-a413f395b492",
                                        "entry_id": "b028ba15-d4ce-3b90-938b-60b7342d8bd7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "For a given function, the set of all elements of the domain that are mapped into a given subset of the codomain; (formally) given a function ƒ : X → Y and a subset B ⊆ Y, the set ƒ⁻¹(B) = {x ∈ X : ƒ(x) ∈ B}."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "175d0626-bbcf-43d6-aff8-1c904b9d8ece",
                                                "sense_id": "57f30964-aa7b-34fb-816b-a413f395b492",
                                                "text": "The preimage of #92;#123;4,9#92;#125; under the function f#58;#92;R#92;rightarrow#92;R#58;f(x)#61;x² is the set #92;#123;-3,-2,#43;2,#43;3#92;#125;."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3de5b414-7e67-46a6-9a49-2564c94785a2",
                                                "sense_id": "57f30964-aa7b-34fb-816b-a413f395b492",
                                                "text": "1967 [Academic Press], Francois Treves, Topological Vector Spaces, Distributions and Kernels, 2006, Dover, page 22,\nThe preimage of a neighborhood U of 0 in E must be a neighborhood of (0,x), since (0,x) is mapped into 0."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0e377505-a7ff-4872-8aae-b7c1cab1a9cf",
                                                "sense_id": "57f30964-aa7b-34fb-816b-a413f395b492",
                                                "text": "Previously, maps and hypermaps were constructed as the preimages of a segment joining two of the three critical values."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6bdd390b-a282-4c3c-909e-0eeea446e91b",
                                                "sense_id": "57f30964-aa7b-34fb-816b-a413f395b492",
                                                "text": "Loosely speaking, saying that a function f is one-way implies that given y (in the range of f) it is infeasible to find a preimage of y under f."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
